We can interpret such a solution as a scattering of plane waves for which the coefficient T± plays the
role of the transmission coefficient. Being the total momentum a conserved quantity, the two particles
can only exchange their momenta, as expected from a theory in one-dimension. Furthermore, for each
value k of the relative momentum, the two particles can also acquire an additional phase of π. As the
interaction is a compact perturbation of the free evolution, the continuous spectrum is the same as
that of the free walk. Eq. (18) provides the generalized eigenvector if U2 (χ, p) corresponding to the
continuous spectrum σc = Ω++   f  ∪ Ω+−f .


